"""Hand-authored problem and reading decisions for the fully read Pro corpus."""
import csv,json
from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
items={int(x['id'].split('-')[-1]):x for x in json.loads((R/'SA21_items.json').read_text())}
problems=[]
def P(n,title,ids,as_he,plausible,art,why,worth,refs,claim_ids):
 problems.append({'id':f'MP-SA21-{n:03d}','title':title,'occurrences':[{'item':items[i]['id'],'cote':items[i]['cote'],'scans':items[i]['scans'],'lines':items[i]['lines']} for i in ids],'as_he_states':as_he,'plausible_today':plausible,'state_of_art':'As of 19 November 2026. '+art,'why_open':why,'worth':worth,'refs':refs,'claims':claim_ids})
P(1,'A recursive notion of form',[1,2,20,37],'The opening proposes recursion in dimension and repeatedly revises the data for dimension one. The cancelled axiom list is explicitly provisional.','A category of objects with a specified equivalence notion, together with a theorem for a fixed tame or conically smooth class; not a universal topological reconstruction from bare incidence.','Malgoire2002 describes the figure-first program; AFR2019 Theorem4.2.5 gives a fully faithful exit construction under its conically smooth stratified-homotopy conventions. Neither supplies the missing induction for these definitions.','The source proceeds through successive axiom systems; it gives no historical explanation of why the general program was not completed.','Worth a later comparison only after the final atelier vocabulary and its retained data have been fixed.',['Malgoire2002','AFR2019'],['C-SA21-1-002','C-SA21-1-034'])
P(2,'Do support and endpoints determine a segment?',[18,22],'He asks whether injectivity is an extra assumption, then states support injectivity in Corollary4.','F1 and the side-containment/separation clauses of F2 already imply support injectivity and endpoint uniqueness among subsegments of a fixed segment.','The complete elementary proof is R-SA21-support. Hua2026 Theorem76.1 contains the corresponding local uniqueness step in a single-segment model; no novelty claim is made.','Resolved within the author’s sequence by an asserted corollary; the written proof is abbreviated, not an abandoned global problem.','Worth incorporating as a short proved lemma in the companion, not a new research task.',['Hua2026'],['C-SA21-1-008','C-SA21-1-009','C-SA21-1-010'])
P(3,'What strengthening does the branch axiom need?',[5,10],'The margin says to complete F3, and the later local filtering statement depends on it.','Common nontrivial initial subsegments must be downward filtered inside a fixed segment; then the intended germ relation is transitive.','R-SA21-branches checks this step. Hua2026 Theorem76.1 explicitly includes the reconstructed F3′ but excludes general branch transitivity from its claimed formalized scope.','The source itself notices the omitted condition. The exact wording of the marginal formula remains a scan question.','Worth settling in a fixed axiom-version table before checking any global orientation theorem.',['Hua2026'],['C-SA21-1-011','C-SA21-1-019'])
P(4,'Which tame-arc examples satisfy F5 or F6?',[24],'The proposed embedded-arc example is followed by an unfinished qualification about F5 or F6.','Choose an exact PL or semialgebraic category; verify each axiom separately, and impose one-dimensional branch conditions when asking for exactly two germs.','The open-primary regular-neighborhood literature and Esquisse discuss controlled local geometry, but neither is an exact theorem about this F1–F6 list. Plane arc germs already show that two-branch behavior is additional.','The qualifying sentence is unfinished; no explanation beyond the working revision is supplied.','Worth a compact example audit once the intended tame category is stated.',['Esquisse1984','SiebenmannGuillouHahl1973'],['C-SA21-1-016'])
P(5,'Which restrictions preserve a network?',[25,26,27,29],'After asking the question in a cancelled draft, he asserts inheritance under every subset.','Require at least closure under all ambient F4 composites needed by retained pieces; order-convex subsets work in the checked interval models.','R-SA21-restriction refutes arbitrary inheritance, including strengthened ambient F1–F5 and both F6 versions. Hua2026 expressly does not prove the restriction claim.','The source asserts an answer without checking the gap created by nonexhaustive gluing. It does not give a later explanation of this failure.','Worth a bounded theorem on admissible restrictions; the unrestricted question is already answered negatively.',['Hua2026'],['C-SA21-1-017','C-SA21-1-018'])
P(6,'Quotients of the singular lieux',[19],'He announces an equivalence relation on singular lieux as a future enrichment after cancelling the critical-locus version.','Specify the equivalence relation and require compatibility with supports, endpoint pairs and branch germs before forming a quotient network.','No exact quotient theorem was located in the recorded native-vocabulary searches; Malgoire2002 confirms only the broader figure-based setting.','The relevant definition is deferred and no compatibility axioms are supplied in these cotes.','Worth retaining as a definition-design question, with low priority until a realization target is fixed.',['Malgoire2002'],['C-SA21-1-033'])
P(7,'Coherent global orientation',[30,31,97],'For a connected regular model, all endpoint and branch pairs should identify coherently into exactly two orientation classes; he says that if false it must become an axiom.','For a specified final axiom list, prove the transport graph is connected and every closed transport has trivial permutation holonomy, or exhibit a model with nontrivial holonomy.','R-SA21-orientation isolates the exact elementary obstruction. Hua2026 Theorem76.1 handles only one ambient segment and explicitly leaves the global orientation question aside. No exact resolution was located.','He explicitly says the verification is absent and proposes an additional axiom if the statement fails.','High-value bounded task: normalize transports and test the first possible closed configurations.',['Hua2026'],['C-SA21-1-024','C-SA21-1-025'])
P(8,'Local versus global directedness',[33,34],'The attempted proof of global filtering from local filtering and connectedness is withdrawn.','The local assumptions alone do not suffice; add a common-extension condition of the appropriate F6 strength.','The rational ordinal-sum fork is completely checked in R-SA21-fork. It also explains exactly where F6 fails. No external theorem is needed to negate the original implication.','The author identifies a counterexample on the pages and introduces F6 immediately afterward.','Useful as a warning lemma in the companion; no further run is needed for the negative answer.',['Hua2026'],['C-SA21-1-027','C-SA21-1-028','C-SA21-1-029'])
P(9,'Equal-endpoint segments and the circular case',[17,35,36,97],'An early cancelled draft predicts a circle-type two-segment exception; later he claims endpoint branch distinction and intersection only at the endpoints, but stops mid-proof.','Separate the endpoint-branch lemma under strong F6 from the weak-F6 version with singular endpoints, then complete the intersection proof before proposing a classification.','The source-specific search located no exact completion; Hua2026 explicitly excludes these last pages. The earlier run’s weak/strong-F6 counterexample refutes an equivalence, not the equal-endpoint lemma itself.','The supplied last page ends in an unfinished formula; the early classification belongs to a cancelled version.','High interest, moderate one-run reach: settle the precise two-segment lemma before any global classification.',['Hua2026'],['C-SA21-1-003','C-SA21-1-031','C-SA21-1-032'])
P(10,'Which endpoints must F6 retain?',[8,23,35],'The first F6 explicitly omits the other endpoints; a later reformulation includes whole supports, and the equal-endpoint argument refuses to assume the other endpoint regular.','Treat interior extension and support extension as different axioms. The regular-end reduction is conditional on a proved prolongation lemma.','The two-maxima rational order in R-SA21-weakF6 verifies strict inequivalence at singular ends. Hua2026 excludes F6 and the general F4 prolongation corollaries.','The source itself states the endpoint limitation; the later alleged equivalence does not resolve its interaction with singular ends.','Worth checking the general prolongation corollary and then the special singular-end use in one focused run.',['Hua2026'],['C-SA21-1-013','C-SA21-1-014','C-SA21-1-029','C-SA21-1-031'])
P(11,'Topological realization of abstract réseaux',[37,79,81,82,83,84,85,86,90],'The title of coteII announces topological realizations, but the package develops constructions rather than a realization-and-recovery equivalence.','Fix a source category, a target category and whether the comparison is homeomorphism, isotopy, weak equivalence or stratified homotopy. Retain attaching and link data appropriate to that choice.','McCord1966 Theorems1–3 concern weak homotopy; BarmakMinian2008 Theorem3.10 concerns simple equivalence; Clader2009 Theorem1.1 and corrected Lemma3.1 concern a specific barycentric inverse system and quotient; Stacks09XV obstructs a connected Hausdorff space as a raw finite-space inverse limit. None proves the proposed literal cutting-system equivalence.','No such theorem is formulated in the supplied early pages; the finite-pro-object suggestion belongs to the earlier planning context.','High conceptual interest but a few-hour run should only specify and test one exact comparison.',['McCord1966','BarmakMinian2008','Clader2009','Clader2016','Stacks09XV','AFR2019'],['C-SA21-1-034'])
P(12,'Regularity after cutting and the cone lemma',[47,48,49],'He acknowledges that the first cut-piece argument lacks regular immersion, adds Li c, and invokes a two-cone lemma.','Require the two closed pieces to cover a neighborhood in the cone lemma. For contractions, keep the ends disjoint from the contracted set or recheck all quotient end conditions.','R-SA21-cone-cover supplies the cover repair. R-SA21-side-contraction gives a connected finite tripod counterexample to the literal side-contained contraction claim. The PL regularity subcase is proved by a height-function construction.','The first missing condition is explicit. The further cover and contraction defects are mathematical diagnostics of the literal transcript, not claims about the ink.','Worth a short corrected local-cutting theorem in a fixed PL category.',['SiebenmannGuillouHahl1973','Esquisse1984'],['C-SA21-2-012','C-SA21-2-015','C-SA21-2-017'])
P(13,'What is the category in the cutting equivalence?',[51,52,53,97],'The pages announce equivalences between cut ficelles and lists of pieces with specified gluing homeomorphisms, without giving morphisms.','For compact Hausdorff admissible pieces, choose endpoint-preserving homeomorphisms compatible with every gluing map and prove cutting/pushout are quasi-inverse; treat isotopy quotients separately.','Esquisse §5 describes boundary-attaching pushouts and their noncanonical choices. The closed-pushout reassociation and n+1/n count are checked here, but no published exact equivalence in this ficelle language was located.','The manuscript labels the construction for memory and passes to the order on lieux rather than writing a full categorical proof.','High one-run reach for a precise elementary theorem once morphisms and admissibility are fixed.',['Esquisse1984'],['C-SA21-2-018','C-SA21-2-019','C-SA21-2-020','C-SA21-2-021'])
P(14,'Connected ends, existence and interval classification',[55,56,57,58,77,78],'He claims existence precisely for connected nontrivial compact triangulable spaces, proposes connected or contractible ends, and gives an interval classification.','For reduced structures on connected finite polyhedra, existence and unique-pair iff arc are true. The unrestricted necessity and interval classification require correction; endpoint connectedness repairs the latter.','R-SA21-disconnected and R-SA21-interval refute the two broader assertions. R-SA21-polyhedra proves the reduced statements. The native ficelle formulation was not located in the searched literature; standard noncut-point theory is comparison background, not a substituted theorem.','The source itself notices a connectivity issue when retracting endpoint enlargement but does not explicitly retract all neighboring assertions.','Worth publishing precise companion lemmas and counterexamples; no broad new classification is warranted.',['Malgoire2002','Crainic2024'],['C-SA21-2-023','C-SA21-2-024','C-SA21-2-025','C-SA21-2-026','C-SA21-2-027'])
P(15,'Equivalence classes of oriented ficelle structures',[59],'He conjectures that all oriented structures on a triangulable X are equivalent under endpoint inclusions, then rejects this already for a segment.','Classify only after fixing the permitted end category and the exact inclusion-generated equivalence. The connected-end interval subfamily has two orientations.','The author’s own retraction and R-SA21-interval settle the naive claim; no exact general replacement was found. A graph of permitted decorated pairs is a possible finite test model, not an established theorem.','He explicitly writes that the conjecture is false already in the segment case.','Moderate interest; a few-hour run can classify the interval or a small graph under a chosen end restriction.',['Crainic2024'],['C-SA21-2-028'])
P(16,'Property-P restrictions on cuts and links',[60,61,62,63],'He suggests imposing P on ends, internal loci and both relevant conic links to preserve the cutting-order properties.','Give sufficient closure conditions on P and the allowed embeddings; distinguish point, ball, sphere and bicollared examples rather than one unrestricted assertion for every intrinsic P.','Esquisse §5 discusses tame gluing and stronger isotopy comparisons; AFR2019 Theorem4.2.5 requires conically smooth stratified data. These are partial frameworks, not a proof for arbitrary P.','The source offers examples and a recommended hypothesis, without a uniform theorem or proof.','High interest, low general one-run reach; begin with one concrete PL property and an explicit interpolation construction.',['Esquisse1984','AFR2019'],['C-SA21-2-029','C-SA21-2-030','C-SA21-2-031','C-SA21-2-032','C-SA21-2-033'])
P(17,'The rive comparison and the implicit relative spectrum',[64,65,66,67,68,69,70],'The page identifies the rive algebra with clopens of a conic link, asserts a section formula under paracompactness, and writes an implicit Spec producing a boundary over A.','Use closed A in paracompact Hausdorff X and the relative Stone space of (i_*F₂)|A. Keep this distinct from the global ring spectrum and the étalé space of elements.','Schapira2023 Proposition10.1.2(iii) is the exact section theorem, tracing to Godement1958. Gillam2011 supplies relative ringed-space localization; Hua2026 Theorem77.1 verifies the explicit rive-to-boundary injection. The cylinder calculation separates the three possible spaces.','The spectrum base/topology is left implicit; the ordinary sheaf theorem itself is not an unresolved new problem.','Worth a precise convention and comparison lemma before constructing full cuts.',['Schapira2023','Godement1958','Gillam2011','Hua2026'],['C-SA21-2-035','C-SA21-2-036','C-SA21-2-037','C-SA21-2-038','C-SA21-2-039','C-SA21-2-040'])
P(18,'Regularity of the full spectral cut',[71],'He asserts regular-immersion transfer to the full boundary and its clopen parts; a doubtful reciprocal margin may ask for a converse.','First define the full cut as a topological space over X and prove the relevant quotient and properness facts; then formulate forward transfer and any converse separately.','Gillam2011 makes a possible relative spectrum precise, but does not prove this ficelle regularity-transfer assertion. Hua2026 explicitly leaves the cut construction outside its formalized result.','The notation is implicit and the reciprocal margin is uncertain; no historical reason beyond this source gap is documented.','High foundational interest; one run can handle compact conic or finite PL examples and formulate the exact theorem.',['Gillam2011','Hua2026'],['C-SA21-2-042'])
P(19,'When does the partial cut characterize a maille?',[72,73],'A maille with one node and a birive should be equivalent to the corresponding partial cut being a ficelle.','For a fixed full-cut model, verify regularity and quotient-connectedness transfer for both birive pieces and both copies of A; exclude inadmissible trivial or empty boundary pieces through the stated end conditions.','Boolean duality constructs complementary clopens; it does not alone supply the topology or connectivity of the glued space. Gillam2011 and Esquisse give relevant constructions, not this exact iff theorem.','The manuscript says the equivalence is evident but supplies no proof of the required transfers.','Worth a dedicated compact-conic test after the full-cut definition is settled.',['Gillam2011','Esquisse1984','Stone1934'],['C-SA21-2-043','C-SA21-2-044'])
P(20,'May the nodes always be reduced to points?',[74],'A marginal note suggests reducing A1,A2 to points, with uncertain wording and scope.','Use point reduction only for statements invariant under the particular closed quotient, and explicitly preserve the necessary side and regularity data.','The finite tripod checks show that contractions involving ends can lose ficelle conditions. No blanket reduction theorem was found in the native searches or the opened relative-spectrum source.','The margin is incomplete and its attachment is uncertain; this is not a proved instruction applying retroactively to every definition.','Useful as a checklist within the cut theorem; low value as an independent general project until its scope is recovered from the scan.',['Gillam2011'],['C-SA21-2-045'])
P(21,'Symmetry of two-node maille compatibility',[75,97],'The final page asserts that the condition relating two disjoint nodes and their two birives is symmetric.','In a fixed cut model, formulate the four retained side germs and show successive cuts commute under precisely stated connectivity and regularity conditions.','No exact resolution was found in the recorded searches. Ordinary pushout associativity addresses only part of the problem; it does not supply the missing compatibility of birives or connected components for disconnected nodes.','The supplied text ends immediately after the assertion, without proof; the last formula has a set-difference reading uncertainty.','High one-run value after the cut model is fixed; test small graphs and disconnected-node examples first.',['Esquisse1984','Gillam2011'],['C-SA21-2-046'])
P(22,'Abstract disjoint lieux versus geometric compatible cuts',[22,50,53,80],'The abstract definition postpones disjoint lieux; coteII develops a stronger-than-set-disjointness geometric compatibility and an order.','Construct an explicit comparison preserving the defined segment and cut relations; do not identify them solely because both use the word disjoint.','The alternating circle example in R-SA21-incompatible separates set disjointness from geometric compatibility. The later pair-cut/betweenness theorem in Hua2026 §78 concerns its own ordered setting, not an automatic topological realization of every early réseau.','The abstract discussion explicitly says it will return to the notion; the geometric construction is related but no identification is proved in the two cotes.','Worth an exact dictionary on an interval or a restricted graph before any general equivalence.',['Hua2026','Esquisse1984'],['C-SA21-1-009','C-SA21-2-019','C-SA21-2-021'])
(R/'parts/root/problems.json').write_text(json.dumps(problems,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))

